# MetaG: UMA-based metabolic reaction thermodynamics

This is the **MetaG_JCIM development copy**. The publication target is a
methods Article in the Journal of Chemical Information and Modeling (JCIM). Its evidence protocol and submission gates
are in [PROTOCOL.txt](analysis/publication_methods_20261008/PROTOCOL.txt).
The starting
package and historical artifacts are preserved in a verified external snapshot.
The earlier development plan is retained in [NCS_DEVELOPMENT.txt](NCS_DEVELOPMENT.txt), with integrated
checks and remaining scientific gates in
[VALIDATION.txt](analysis/ncs_development/VALIDATION.txt). The current CPU suite
is recorded in the dated [implementation status](analysis/publication_hardening_20261009/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.txt); independent chemical-transfer superiority is not established.
The integrated parallel work is summarized in
[IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.txt](analysis/ncs_parallel_integration_20261008/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.txt).
The latest optional solvent-relaxation fixes and bounded quantum experiment are
recorded in [PIPELINE_UPDATE.txt](analysis/ncs_solvent_sampling_20261008/PIPELINE_UPDATE.txt).

MetaG estimates **standard transformed reaction Gibbs energies (ΔrG′°)** from
molecular structures and reaction stoichiometry. It combines a UMA machine-learned
interatomic potential (MLIP) with conformer sampling, thermal corrections,
solvation, protonation corrections, and structural reaction routing.

The aim is a generalizable, physically meaningful workflow for metabolic
thermodynamics. In the default configuration, reaction-energy predictions are
not fitted to openTECR reaction ΔG values. openTECR is used for benchmarking,
method development, and uncertainty calibration. Some auxiliary models use
independent property data, such as hydration equilibria and pKa references.
Benchmark-guided development is not independent external validation.

**Status:** research software and a JCIM methods manuscript in development.
The current checkout is **0.2.0.dev20 / physics 2026-10-10d**.
Source-backed citrate, fumarate and L-malate protonation ladders now apply by
default to exact full-reference structure matches, with source, condition and
ownership checks. No reaction residual selects or fits these parameters.
The neutral ring-proton ensemble remains experimental and disabled: its complete
panel assessment worsened the mean and severe-error tail despite improving the
folate example. Uncertainty artifacts without
the exact estimator/source identity provide diagnostic widths without a coverage
claim. The [release work index](analysis/workflow_release_20261022_01/WORK_STATUS.txt)
records consolidation and final integration checks; the
[physical-study index](analysis/state_workflow_20261021_01/WORK_STATUS.txt) records
accepted/rejected candidates and evidence limits.
The [previous review](analysis/publication_readiness_20261021_01/REVIEW.txt) and
dev18 reviewer wheel remain frozen historical checkpoints.

The dev17 full-panel evaluation is **complete**: **251/364 estimates**, 107
preflight exclusions and six unavailable parent computations. Historical-reference
MAE is **9.251 kJ/mol**, with ten absolute errors over 30 kJ/mol. The source-backed
acid-registry workflow gives 9.174 kJ/mol on the same 251 targets in a verified
public-API warm-cache comparison. These are retrospective,
development-exposed, normalized-reference comparisons. Their source and outcome
identities remain under `analysis/publication_sprint_20261018_01/`; the read-only
reconstruction is `analysis/publication_readiness_20261021_01/baseline_verified.json`.
Cache reuse retains its parent costs and does not establish a cold-start completion
rate. The whole-panel full-molecule candidates were rejected for worse error and
severe-tail behavior. The acid registry improves the declared protonation
representation using independent auxiliary-property sources; its benchmark
result does not establish independent reaction-prediction accuracy.

Dev17 preserves source cysteine stereochemistry in peptide cores and rejects
generic cuts that introduce undefined stereochemistry. The earlier
[17 October review](analysis/pipeline_enhancement_20261017_01/REVIEW.txt) separates
the dev16 source-backed protonation safeguards from numerical evidence. The
source-backed citrate execution check uses dev15. The earlier
[staged release manifest](analysis/publication_revision_20261012_01/release/manifest.json)
records clean CPU installed-source equality and dependency checks. Earlier eight-reaction GPU calculations
use frozen dev14 source on the pinned QM dependency environment; earlier installed
dev13 verification retains its original identity. The
[current work index](analysis/publication_revision_20261012_01/STATUS.md) links
the cold biochemical evaluation, fresh published baselines, chemical identity
admission, AMP diagnostics, network audit and manuscript evidence.
The corrected phosphate-acid stereo gate returns five of eight cold biochemical
estimates; four have admitted conditional labels, with MAE 6.37 kJ/mol versus
1.54 for published eQuilibrator CC on those same four targets. Pretrained exposure,
shared condition transforms and unresolved state/metal conventions prevent an
independent generalization ranking. No reaction residual was fitted.
The [six-system matched comparison](analysis/value_demonstration_20261010/challenge_replica1/REPORT.md)
shows a neutral-domain computational fidelity lead and retains all charged DFT
exclusions. Independent experimental validation, network consistency on the
claimed domain, uncertainty coverage and distribution decisions remain before
submission. The manuscript is not yet submission ready. `../MetaG_NCS` is a compatibility symlink to this
renamed directory so frozen provenance paths remain resolvable. Historical
source manifests and analysis directory names retain their original names.

The optional `--sampling-profile thorough` preset increases search effort
(pools and kept sets doubled; plateau tolerance 0.5 kJ/mol; three hits;
12-batch cap). It must be selected before the QM module is imported.
It has no established experimental accuracy or coverage advantage; validate
independent seed offsets using `metag.tools.audit_sampling`. The legacy
`--seeds` argument does not run independent replicas.

Accepted implicit-ensemble minima now retain their ordered coordinates, atom
symbols, charge/spin, geometry hash and component energies, including minima
whose prior Hessian validation was reused. This enables independent inspection
or QM refinement of the exact accepted conformers. The archive describes sampled
states; it does not establish complete conformer coverage.

Export these accepted states for external inspection with
`python -m metag.tools.export_minima /path/to/species_cache.json -o new_export`.
The tool checks geometry hashes and component closure, preserves XYZ coordinate
precision, and refuses missing geometries or an existing destination directory.
Older energy-only caches cannot be reconstructed by this tool.

For controlled molecular-state diagnostics, use
`python -m metag.tools.compare_states comparison_plan.json -o new_report.json`.
This CPU tool checks frozen cache/result hashes, compares electronic and solvent
substitutions, and recomputes populations for every declared fixed-charge state.
It refuses missing states, inconsistent producers and duplicate state identities.
The same tool also compares atom/charge-balanced component exchanges using a
`stoichiometry` plan, requiring every recorded conformer and zero net molecular
count so omitted reservoir and standard-state terms cannot mimic a correction.
Selected-minimum controls remain distinct from full recorded ensembles. See
[state-ensemble comparisons](docs/state-ensemble-comparisons.md) for the input
contract and limitations; these diagnostics do not change default predictions.

Use `metag tools` to list maintained commands and `metag COMMAND --help` for
their input contracts. For example, `metag preflight reactions.json -o report.json`
and `metag compare-states plan.json -o comparison.json` run without the optional
QM stack. Existing `python -m metag.tools.MODULE` commands remain compatible.
`metag score` and the original `metag --only REACTION` invoke the same scorer.
The dated `analysis/` studies retain frozen inputs/code/results; they are evidence
records rather than additional production entry points.

## Workflow and scope

1. Validate structures, stoichiometry, and elemental/charge balance.
2. Select full molecules or structural cores and construct reference protonation states.
3. Generate conformers, rank and relax them with UMA, and evaluate xTB solvation.
4. Combine electronic energies, thermal corrections, conformer populations,
   standard-state corrections, and enabled aqueous-state contributions.
5. Assemble reaction ΔG, apply protonation terms, and report uncertainty,
   applicability diagnostics, and provenance.

Reported energies are in **kJ/mol for the reaction as written**. Current conditions
are **298.15 K, pH 7, ionic strength 0 M, no Mg²⁺ binding, 1 M solute standard
states, and pure liquid water**. These conditions are fixed in the current
pipeline; arbitrary-condition calculations and concentration-dependent reaction
ΔG are not implemented as user options.

## Installation

Requires Python **3.10+**. From this directory, install the CPU routing library:

```bash
python -m pip install -e .
python -m metag.cli --help
```

Full energy calculations require Python **3.11–3.13** (the tested interpreter is
3.12), and additionally require a compatible UMA/fairchem runtime,
accessible model weights, CUDA, and an xTB executable:

```bash
python -m pip install -e ".[qm,test]"
export XTB_BIN=/absolute/path/to/xtb
```

For the tested Linux/CUDA dependency combination, use
`python -m pip install -c environments/qm-linux-cuda128.txt ".[qm]"`.
The clean-install validation record is under
[publication_hardening_20261009](analysis/publication_hardening_20261009); model
weights and the external xTB binary are provisioned separately.

The constrained clean QM environment is the supported installation record;
historical development runs retain their actual imported dependency versions.
The QM extra fixes fairchem to 2.21.0 and Torch to the 2.8 series, whose
fairchem dependency requires NumPy below 2.5. The reference extra fixes
equilibrator-api to the tested 0.6.0. These are supported API bounds; transitive
dependencies still need the recorded environment for exact reproduction.
Installing Python extras does not install xTB or provision model access.

To fit or assess uncertainty models, install `python -m pip install ".[calibration]"`.
This adds SciPy and scikit-learn for the fitting tools; the packaged inference
models and CPU structural tools do not require those dependencies. Fitting an
uncertainty model does not fit reaction-energy point estimates or establish
external validation. A changed point estimator needs its own reference and
held-out assessment before prediction intervals can be enabled.

For CPU thermochemistry from an already supplied Hessian, install
`python -m pip install ".[thermal]"`. This extra needs NumPy/ASE and imports
neither Torch nor fairchem for supplied-Hessian calculations. Computing a UMA
Hessian still requires the QM runtime and model access.

## Quick start

Create `reactions.json` with a balanced example:

```json
{
  "ester_hydrolysis": {
    "note": "Ethyl acetate hydrolysis",
    "n_Hplus": 0,
    "species": {
      "ethyl_acetate": [-1, 0, "CCOC(C)=O"],
      "water": [-1, 0, "O"],
      "acetate_reference": [1, 0, "CC(=O)O"],
      "ethanol": [1, 0, "CCO"]
    }
  }
}
```

Each species is `[stoichiometric coefficient, formal charge, SMILES]`:
negative coefficients denote reactants, positive coefficients products, and
fractional coefficients are supported. Charges must match the SMILES. Include
water explicitly when required; disconnected mixtures are not single species.
`n_Hplus` follows the same product-positive convention. Optional `exp` values
are reference energies for comparison, not inputs to the point prediction.

Inspect routing on CPU without loading UMA:

```python
import json
from metag.routing.planner import route_reaction

with open("reactions.json") as stream:
    reaction = json.load(stream)["ester_hydrolysis"]
routed, diagnostics, truncated = route_reaction(reaction)
```

Run scoring **inside a reserved GPU allocation**:

```bash
export RXN_FILE="$PWD/reactions.json"
export METAG_OUT="$PWD/metag_out"
export METAG_CACHE="$PWD/.metag_cache/species_cache"
metag --only ester_hydrolysis
```

On the project cluster, launch through `gpu_reserve run <idx> -- metag --only
ester_hydrolysis`, substituting the assigned device index. Do not select a shared
GPU by manually setting `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES`. Omitting `--only` scores every
reaction in `RXN_FILE`; without `RXN_FILE`, the packaged openTECR panel is used.

Alternatively, inside the same allocation, reuse the `reaction` above:

```python
import os
from metag.energetics.uma import load_uma
from metag.pipeline import score_reaction

model = load_uma(os.environ.get("UMA_MODEL", "uma-s-1p2p1"))
result = score_reaction(model, reaction, key="ester_hydrolysis")
print(result["dG"], result["ci95"], result["suspect"])
```

## Configuration and outputs

Set environment variables **before importing the scoring pipeline**.

| Variable | Default / purpose |
|---|---|
| `RXN_FILE` | Packaged openTECR inputs; override with a reaction JSON file |
| `METAG_OUT` | `./metag_out`; CLI result directory |
| `METAG_CACHE` | `./.metag_cache/species_cache`; species-energy cache |
| `CONFORMER_SEED_OFFSET` | `0`; implicit search seed offset; use nonoverlapping windows for repeatability checks |
| `CONV_TOL`, `CONV_HITS`, `CONV_MAX` | `2.5` kJ/mol, `2`, `8`; provisional plateau tolerance, consecutive valid hits and batch cap |
| `THERMAL_VALIDATION_MAX` | `64`; exceeding the sampled-candidate budget withholds the estimate; a larger budget changes configuration and cost |
| `XTB_BIN` | Defaults to `$HOME/miniforge3/envs/xtb/bin/xtb`; set explicitly elsewhere |
| `UMA_MODEL` | `uma-s-1p2p1`; must match the loaded model |
| `UMA_HESSIAN` | `autograd`; `fd` selects the historical finite-difference method |
| `SOLV_MODEL` | `alpb`; implicit solvation model |
| `PKA_MODEL` | `table`; optional `site` model has a restricted chemistry domain |
| `PKA_SITE_MODEL_FILE` | Optional site-model parameter artifact; its hash is reported |
| `SPECIES_CACHE` | `1`; set to `0` to disable cache reuse/writes |
| `METAG_ROUTING_MODE` | `reaction`; `full_molecule` disables structural cores/truncation; `compound_reference` additionally assigns owned references from each compound alone |

Routing defaults are in [config.py](src/metag/config.py). The CLI retains
`--seeds`, `--keep`, and `--pool` for compatibility; implicit-species sampling
uses structural budgets and adaptive convergence rather than those values alone.

The CLI checkpoints a JSON list after every reaction to
`METAG_OUT/unified_pipeline_<key-or-all>.json`.

| Result field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `dG`, `dG_raw` | Reported and unanchored routed-reaction energies; neither is simply an electronic-energy difference |
| `sigma_pred`, `ci95` | Available only when calibration applies at 95%; otherwise `null`, `[null, null]` |
| `uncertainty_diagnostic` | Historical width and scale, retained without asserted coverage |
| `unresolved`, `sign_status` | `unresolved=null` means sign uncertainty is unavailable; it does not establish a confident sign |
| `routes.protonation_diagnostics` | Per-term pKa contributions and analytic pKa/pH sensitivities; structural model gaps |
| `U_samp` | Propagated spread of dependent provisional sampling estimates; not a calibrated sampling standard error or an unsampled-state bound |
| `ci_info.coverage_calibrated` | Whether the interval meets the implementation's calibration/applicability checks |
| `suspect`, `routes` | Failure reason and routing/coverage diagnostics |
| `stages`, `species_scored` | Energy contributions and structures actually evaluated |
| `conditions`, `config` | Thermodynamic conditions and estimator configuration |

Historical development version `0.2.0.dev11` uses physics revision `2026-10-10d`.
Dev8 withholds uncalibrated prediction intervals and sign classifications.
Consumers must handle unavailable uncertainty explicitly; historical numeric
widths remain under `uncertainty_diagnostic`. It also records independent-site
pKa/pH sensitivities to support accuracy diagnosis. These changes do not alter
default point-estimator equations. Macroscopic pKas cannot be substituted for
microscopic parameters without establishing the reference-state mapping.
Dev6 added the optional full-molecule routing preset and a CPU panel gate; the
default point-estimator equations are unchanged. Routing policy and source
identities distinguish this interface from the sealed earlier calculations.
Dev7 adds the optional `compound_reference` mode. It uses the existing pKa
provider consistently for each compound, keeps permanent ions charged, records
proton uptake and per-compound acid/base terms, and verifies element/charge
balance. Unsupported references fail without changing the other compounds'
reference path. Flat user-supplied pKa terms are refused because they lack
compound ownership. Class constants and independent-site assumptions retain
their limitations; this mode has not established an accuracy improvement.
Reaction scoring now checks original and routed stereochemical identity before
energy evaluation. Unspecified tetrahedral centers, double bonds, and relative
stereoisomer groups cannot silently become conformer ensembles. A
resonance-normalized copy prevents equivalent nonbridging phosphate oxygens from
creating false stereocenters; it does not alter the QM input. Newly stereogenic
neutral phosphate reference states may still require an explicit state model
and are withheld when unresolved. Never add arbitrary stereo tags to bypass this check.

Revision `2026-10-10c` added minimum recovery.
After an identity-preserving FIRE failure at the final long thermal retry, a
bounded LBFGS fallback tries the same starting geometry and Hamiltonian.
Admission still requires the force threshold, molecular identity and strict
Hessian checks, followed by recomputation of all energy components. The output
retains optimizer trials and costs. This does not guarantee complete sampling. Revision `2026-10-10a` fixed
optimizer endpoint energy/geometry synchronization when the step budget is exhausted
and retains reproducible geometries for failed minimum validation. Revision `2026-10-10b` prevents unusable sampling batches from counting as
plateau evidence and treats nonfinite solvation values as failed calculations.
Valid rediscoveries of known minima still count. Revision
`2026-10-09a` canonicalized molecular atom ordering before implicit and explicit sampling.
Equivalent SMILES therefore use the same finite-search input as their shared
cache identity. Hydration and ring-chain sampling contributions now use
partition-function derivatives (including the hydration-calibration slope), and
repeated identical species estimates cancel before reaction-level sampling
propagation. These are conditional sampling diagnostics, not calibrated
prediction errors; missing-state, solvent, pKa and fitted-parameter errors remain
outside this propagation. This changes sampling for some inputs and requires fresh
numerical validation; the sealed `2026-10-07e` pilot is a separate estimator.

From `0.2.0.dev1`, configuration identity includes API routing overrides and
whether the radius was selected automatically or explicitly. Species caches
also include the raw-species producer source hash, backend versions, xTB executable hash,
and the loaded UMA checkpoint, reference-energy and inference-setting hashes.
Loaded module versions are recorded separately from distribution metadata, which
can disagree in a modified environment.
The full estimator source is fingerprinted separately; downstream reference/audit
edits do not invalidate unchanged raw-species computations. Older entries without
this producer identity are not reused by live scoring.
Keep historical cache replay in its sealed historical environment.
`load_uma()` supplies the model identity automatically. Custom predictor objects
without a complete `metag_provenance` identity can be scored, but persistent
species caching and water-reference memoization are disabled. Treat model
weights/settings as immutable after loading; restart after changing installed
code or dependencies. Calibration artifacts must be derived from the actual
scored configuration, including its loaded-model identity.

Reference rebuilding defaults to excluding ambiguous compound aliases and
reaction mappings. A condition-screened candidate panel can be produced with:

```bash
python -m pip install -e ".[reference]"
python -m metag.tools.build_reference \
  --source artifacts/benchmark/opentecr_source \
  --modelseed /path/to/ModelSEEDDatabase \
  --reactions src/metag/data/reactions_opentecr_std.json \
  --require-reported-I --temperature-K 298.15 \
  --output new_reference/reactions.json --report new_reference/report.json
```

This screens source conditions and mappings; it does not independently validate
the experimental pool definition, Mg convention, or condition correction.
`--mapping-policy historical_first` deliberately restores alphabetical mapping
for historical reproduction and records that policy in the reference identity.
Unresolved mappings require chemical adjudication before becoming validation
labels. Existing manuscript reference snapshots are preserved separately.

A result with `dG: null` is a failed or unsupported estimate, not zero energy.
An interval with `coverage_calibrated: false` must not be described as validated
95% coverage. Checkpoints preserve outcomes but do not implement job resumption.

## Validation and limitations

The October 6 refactor passed **287 tests**, with **one GPU test skipped**.
All **364 checked routes** and the compared numerical replay fields were
unchanged. These are CPU/mock and historical-cache checks, not fresh validation
of the current autograd estimator. See [validation evidence](analysis/code_review/refactor_validation.json).

Important unresolved limitations include approximate pKa class values and
independent-site treatment of coupled protonation, conformer/solvation errors,
reaction-context-dependent truncation, and incomplete external validation.
The optional site-pKa model rejects unsupported chemistry instead of silently
substituting another model. Legacy reaction-energy anchor corrections remain
disabled. Abandoned **ring-chain code is retained as experimental and disabled**;
its structural tests do not establish aqueous free-energy accuracy.

Current changes require configuration-matched benchmarking and uncertainty
calibration before publication claims. Historical artifacts are preserved;
changing model settings does not validate reuse of their calibrated intervals.

## Tests and development

Run the full CPU/mock suite in the UMA development environment:

```bash
python -m pytest tests -q
```

For a minimal CPU boundary check, install `.[test]` and run
`python -m pytest tests/unit/test_refactor_boundaries.py -q`.
Full-suite collection still requires QM Python dependencies in some modules.
To run integration tests **within a reserved GPU allocation**:

```bash
METAG_RUN_GPU_TESTS=1 python -m pytest -m gpu tests/integration -q
```

Keep refactoring separate from scientific changes. Preserve input/output
contracts, cache identity, routing, and numerical behavior with regression
checks. Scientific changes need independent physical evidence and validation;
do not introduce reaction-specific offsets or tune pKas to benchmark residuals.
When reporting a problem, include a minimal reaction JSON, configuration,
dependency versions, and the failure log/result.

