## Citation and licensing

No project-specific `CITATION.cff` or standalone license file is currently
included in this directory. Publication citation metadata remains to be added.
For reproducibility, record the source revision, configuration, input/reference
snapshots, model identity, and cache provenance. Attribution and licensing
information for bundled reference data is recorded with the artifacts;
third-party software and model weights have their own terms.

Before a ModelSEED batch, run the CPU-only structural preflight:

```bash
python -m metag.tools.preflight reactions.json -o preflight.json
```

The report flags known pKa/speciation gaps in inputs and routed cores; passing
this diagnostic does not validate accuracy. Production routing rejects legacy
anchor, named-polyacid, and hydro-lyase water corrections. See the
[generalization safeguards and remaining work](docs/scientific-policy-audit.md).

Native checkpoints can also be audited without changing their energies:

```bash
python -m metag.tools.audit_network results.json -o network_audit.json
```

The audit keeps failures and unknown metadata visible and reports cycle and
representation inconsistencies. Explicit isotope thermochemistry is unsupported.
The legacy `SMD_SOLV` reaction correction is refused; the strict SMD component
API remains experimental and is not automatically applied to reactions.

Before evaluating a reaction network, check shared-species representations on
CPU. Use the same routing mode for preflight and scoring:

```bash
METAG_ROUTING_MODE=full_molecule python -m metag.tools.audit_routes \
  reactions.json -o route_panel.json --require-consistent
METAG_ROUTING_MODE=full_molecule metag --require-consistent-panel
```

The `full_molecule` preset takes precedence over the four structural-reduction
switches and is recorded in the effective routing policy. It retains the
declared protonation, hydration and solvation models. The CLI writes its panel
report and refuses a failed gate before importing/loading the QM runtime.
Unmapped labels, missing routes, context-dependent shared representations and
known structural exclusions cannot pass. A passing check establishes known
structural consistency within that panel, not energy-cycle closure or valid
aqueous pools. Full-molecule calculations can cost more and still hit sampling
budgets. On the historical 364-reaction inputs, full molecules remove the
largest sugar-cycle structural residual but leave 17 shared compounds with
context-dependent protonation references. Those inputs also retain unresolved
stereochemistry; the panel is refused. Do not use its reaction energies as a
globally coherent network without resolving these state/reference issues.

For a reference-layer consistency check, use
`METAG_ROUTING_MODE=compound_reference` with the same commands. The audit also
checks owned acid/base terms and proton bookkeeping, including shared compounds
with identical scored structures but different reference constants. On 358
historical reactions with constructible references, this mode gives 436 owned
compound references with zero context-dependent models. Six unsupported
reactions and the stereochemistry exclusions remain visible; the full panel
is refused. A separate reference-only test closes 49 cycle sums for structures,
proton counts and acid/base corrections. It computes no new QM energies and
does not prove aqueous-state accuracy or full-energy cycle closure.

## Independent sampling audit and chemical references

Use `python -m metag.tools.audit_sampling` to compare explicitly expected
independent-seed results. The audit checks identical chemistry, conditions,
producer/configuration and nonoverlapping implicit seed windows. Missing or failed
replicates remain in the denominator. A declared equilibrium-ratio tolerance can
be used as a downstream gate with `--require-within-tolerance`; two agreeing
searches do not establish global convergence or confidence coverage. See the
[worked CLI contract](analysis/physical_improvement_20261010/SAMPLING_AUDIT_USAGE.md).

The separate [chemical-reference converter](src/metag/validation/chemical_reference.py)
handles explicit ionic chemical reactions, proton-count populations and molal/molar
standard states. The [AMP hydrolysis example](analysis/chemical_reference_20261010/README.md)
derives a conditional reference from evaluated source data, preserves parameter
sensitivities and distinguishes source uncertainty from total uncertainty. It
does not modify bundled benchmark labels or production protonation rules.

## Reproducible examples and reference screening

[AMP hydrolysis](examples/amp_hydrolysis.json) and
[citrate to D-threo-isocitrate](examples/citrate_isocitrate.json) have explicit
inputs and documented development runs. See [example instructions](examples/README.md)
for CPU preflight and the scope of the recorded results. They contain no
experimental labels used in scoring.

Reference rebuilding also flags pMg values numerically matching explicit added
Mg-salt concentrations. Such rows are excluded until free activity has source
support; total concentration is not automatically free activity. Source-verified
rows can record `p_mg_basis: "free_activity"` with a nonempty `p_mg_source`.
This is a narrow ambiguity screen, not complete experimental curation.

The package remains a development release. Supported molecular identity and
numerical completion are checked separately from experimental accuracy. The
current intervals are not independently calibrated for this revision.

## CPU thermochemistry from a supplied Hessian

Install `python -m pip install ".[thermal]"` to use the RRHO utility with NumPy
and ASE, without Torch, fairchem, model weights or a GPU. The utility
`metag.energetics.thermal.gibbs_corr_from_hessian` expects a Cartesian Hessian in
eV/Å² and electronic energy in eV. Use `return_info=True`, check `n_imag` and
finite output, and retain the declared geometry, symmetry number, spin and
standard states. The calculation uses the documented 50 cm⁻¹ handling and floor.
A returned correction does not establish ensemble completeness or experimental
accuracy.

Dev10 also returns `info["thermodynamics"]`: the ideal-gas enthalpy correction,
entropy, Gibbs correction, ZPE, and internal/floored-mode counts. The components
use the same projected modes and floor as the existing point calculation.
When qRRHO is enabled, its entropy correction is included and harmonic enthalpy
is retained, so `Gcorr = Hcorr - T*S` remains explicit.

For a phase-matched supplied-Hessian check, pass `temperature_K=298.16` and
`pressure_Pa=101325.0` explicitly. These arguments affect this gas utility;
they do not change the reaction pipeline's temperature, solution model, or
state populations. Default reaction point equations remain unchanged. Gas
enthalpy and gas Gibbs references must not be treated as aqueous reaction labels.

The dev5 import change preserves the physical equations. On the same saved DFT
water Hessian, it reproduces the correction exactly while avoiding MLIP imports;
the paired import/check observation dropped from 7.63 to 1.01 seconds. This is an
individual integration timing observation, not a general throughput guarantee.

## Complete sampled-ensemble solvent refinement

Dev12 supplies a CPU tool for replacing the ALPB solvent term on every recorded
accepted conformer and recomputing the Boltzmann partition:

```bash
python -m metag.tools.refine_ensemble species_cache.json \
  --records smd_results -o new_refinement.json
```

Supply one complete strict SMD result per exact ordered geometry. The tool checks
coordinates, charge/spin, component-energy closure, SCF convergence, CDS accounting,
and a common functional, basis, numerical protocol and producer. Missing, failed,
duplicate, or incompatible required records withhold the refinement. Solvent-relaxed
caches, approximate thermal ensembles and mismatched original partitions are refused.
The output retains source/result hashes and recomputed populations; it never
reuses the old uncertainty or overwrites an existing destination.

This is a fixed-geometry refinement of the sampled set. It cannot establish missing
SMD conformers, solvent-optimized minima, population completeness or experimental
accuracy. It does not change the default solvent model.

When every candidate changes molecular identity, dev12 also honors disabled
neutral-reference routing switches: the scorer returns no estimate instead of
silently changing to a pKa-based reference. Permitted bounded retries retain the
original failure and requested-state provenance. Supplied pKa sites are preserved.

Dev13 narrows the rearrangement retry to an explicitly enabled neutral guard or
a remaining zwitterion covered by its enabled guard. Automatic PH0 routing has
already applied its declared isomerase/base gates; a failed geometry does not
override those choices. A zwitterion switch cannot authorize a pure anion to
change reference. Sampled connectivity failures do not prove that the physical
state has no minimum. Existing successful-state numerical equations and default
pKa/solvent values are retained.
